---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<Base title={`about - ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="What this page is, and how it's made.">
    <p class="eyebrow">about this page</p>
    <h1>Show and tell</h1>

    <p>
        I build a lot of small software - apps for my family, tools that run my homelab, the odd
        experiment that got far enough to be worth keeping. Most of it runs privately behind my own
        sign-in, so there's usually nothing for anyone else to click into. This page is where I can
        still show what I've made: what each thing is, why it exists, and roughly how it works.
    </p>

    <p>
        It's the sibling of <a href="https://fisher.sh">fisher.sh</a> and my blog at{' '}
        <a href="https://log.fisher.sh">log.fisher.sh</a> - same typefaces, same palette, same
        general feel. Where a project's code is public you'll find a <em>source</em> link; where it
        isn't, the write-up is the whole story.
    </p>

    <h2>This site is made by agents</h2>
    <p>
        The banner up top isn't a gimmick: this whole site - the copy, the structure, the
        screenshots - is written and maintained by the AI agents I have working on my projects. When
        one of them ships a change to an app, it's supposed to come back here and update that
        project's entry to match. I read behind them, but I don't hand-write these pages.
    </p>
    <p>
        Which means: treat it as a friendly, roughly-accurate tour, not a spec sheet. An agent can
        describe a thing well and still get a detail wrong, or lag a version behind what's actually
        running. I'd rather have that out in the open than pretend a machine wrote nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>How current is this?</h2>
    <p>
        Each project page carries a small <code>snapshot</code> line noting the commit its
        description and screenshots reflect, and when they were captured. It's the honest answer to
        "how far behind might this be?" - if a project moved fast, its snapshot may trail the live
        thing by a bit.
    </p>

    <h2>Spot something off?</h2>
    <p>
        If a description reads as wrong, oversold, or just stale, that's worth knowing - it usually
        means an agent drifted from reality. It'll get corrected. Nothing here is trying to impress
        you; it's a casual record of things I've built.
    </p>

    <p class="back"><a href="/">&larr; back to projects</a></p>
</Base>

<style>
    .back {
        font-family: 'DraftingMono', ui-monospace, monospace;
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        margin-top: 2.5rem;
    }
</style>
